From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roman Leventov <leventov.ru@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 16:15:21 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 26/28] deployment: pace isolate analysis through elastic
 capacity

Module analysis creates one isolate request per non-dependency module, evaluates its import graph in V8 without supported asynchronous ops, and can keep up to ANALYZE_CONCURRENCY attempts active per analysis call. Rejected-before-execution and overload responses can also create bounded retries. This work bypasses application action limits while still consuming shared-base isolate workers, active-JavaScript permits, CPU, and queue entries.

Make the degradable-query leader semaphore an application-scoped admission object shared with isolate analysis. Preserve immediate typed deferral for degradable cache-miss leaders, but make each isolate module attempt wait fairly for one permit before queue admission. This keeps degradable leaders plus active analysis attempts within the configured elastic cap without reserving an idle worker. When the cap is unset, analysis retains its previous behavior.

Transfer each reservation into the queued Analyze request and return it with the terminal response. Queue discard releases it, while dispatched work retains it until the analysis attempt produces its terminal result even if its caller or sibling analysis future is canceled. The client releases a delivered reservation before retry matching and backoff, so sleeping retries do not occupy capacity.

A queued analysis waiter receives released capacity before a later immediate degradable acquisition, providing deployment progress whenever existing permit holders and lower execution resources complete. Normal queries, dependencies, mutations, actions, Node analysis, configuration evaluation, worker eligibility, and dependency reserve remain unchanged.

Expose analysis reservation occupancy and gate-wait metrics. Add focused fairness, capacity-transfer, caller-cancellation, and sibling-cancellation tests. Document patch composition, source evidence, per-call fan-out, sizing and telemetry limits, rollout, rollback, and a controlled deployment experiment.
